# Architecture — custom store, not Redux/Zustand

## Context

`app/src/store.ts` (`createStore` -> `{ getState, dispatch, subscribe }`) is
the only state engine in this app — a hand-rolled store, not Redux/Zustand.
See `materials/architecture-brief.md`.

## Rule

- State changes ONLY through `store.dispatch(action)`. Never mutate the object
  returned by `store.getState()`, and never assign to its fields.
- To add new behavior: add a variant to the `Action` union in
  `app/src/types.ts` (allowed — see `do-not-touch.mdc` for the exact boundary)
  → handle it in `app/src/reducer.ts` (pure, returns a new object) → add a
  matching creator in `app/src/actions.ts` (see `action-creators.mdc`).
- Build actions with the creators from `app/src/actions.ts`, never by hand-
  writing `{ type: "...", payload: {...} }` at the call site.
- Do NOT import or add `redux`, `zustand`, `mobx`, `jotai`, or any other state
  library (see `dependencies.mdc`). Do NOT reimplement `dispatch`/`subscribe`/
  `getState` in a new file — `app/src/store.ts` is the only instance.
- `subscribe` returns an unsubscribe function; call it when a listener should
  stop receiving updates instead of leaving it registered forever (see the
  "stops notifying after unsubscribe" case in `app/src/store.test.ts`).
- UI/consumer code reads state via `app/src/selectors.ts`, not by reaching
  into `state.tasks`/`state.filter` directly.

## How to verify

1. `grep -rniE '"(redux|zustand|mobx|jotai)"[[:space:]]*:' app/package.json`
   returns no matches — redux/zustand/mobx/jotai aren't declared as a
   dependency or devDependency.
2. This syntax-aware check parses each source file's AST (via the
   `typescript` package already installed as a devDependency — no new
   package needed) instead of grepping raw text, so it can't be fooled by a
   mention inside a comment or string literal (e.g. `const note = "x =
   require('redux')"`), while still catching every real import form
   (side-effect `import "redux"`, `from`-form imports/re-exports,
   `require(...)`, dynamic `import(...)`, and subpath specifiers like
   `zustand/vanilla` or `jotai/utils`):
   ```bash
   cd app
   node <<'EOF'
   const ts = require("typescript");
   const fs = require("fs");
   const { execSync } = require("child_process");
   const files = execSync("git ls-files src", { encoding: "utf8" })
     .split("\n")
     .filter((f) => /\.(ts|tsx)$/.test(f));
   const forbidden = /^(redux|zustand|mobx|jotai)(\/.*)?$/;
   const bad = [];
   for (const file of files) {
     const src = fs.readFileSync(file, "utf8");
     const sf = ts.createSourceFile(file, src, ts.ScriptTarget.Latest, true);
     (function visit(node) {
       if (
         (ts.isImportDeclaration(node) || ts.isExportDeclaration(node)) &&
         node.moduleSpecifier &&
         ts.isStringLiteral(node.moduleSpecifier) &&
         forbidden.test(node.moduleSpecifier.text)
       ) {
         bad.push(`${file}: ${node.moduleSpecifier.text}`);
       }
       if (ts.isCallExpression(node)) {
         const isRequire =
           ts.isIdentifier(node.expression) && node.expression.text === "require";
         const isDynImport = node.expression.kind === ts.SyntaxKind.ImportKeyword;
         const arg = node.arguments[0];
         if (
           (isRequire || isDynImport) &&
           arg &&
           ts.isStringLiteral(arg) &&
           forbidden.test(arg.text)
         ) {
           bad.push(`${file}: ${arg.text}`);
         }
       }
       ts.forEachChild(node, visit);
     })(sf);
   }
   if (bad.length) {
     console.log(bad.join("\n"));
     process.exit(1);
   }
   EOF
   ```
   No output (exit 0) means nothing in `app/src` imports, re-exports, or
   requires a state library.
3. Every new action `type` string appears in all three of `types.ts`,
   `reducer.ts`, and `actions.ts`:
   ```bash
   diff <(grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/types.ts | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u) \
        <(grep -oE "case ['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/reducer.ts | grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u)
   diff <(grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/types.ts | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u) \
        <(grep -oE "['\"][a-z]+/[a-z]+['\"]" app/src/actions.ts | tr -d "'\"" | sort -u)
   ```
   Both `diff`s must produce no output — any output means the `Action` union,
   reducer `case` arms, and creators in `actions.ts` have fallen out of sync
   (quote-insensitive, so a variant written with `'single quotes'` is still
   caught).
4. `grep -rnE "getState\(\)(\.[a-zA-Z]+|\[[^]]+\])+\s*=|getState\(\)(\.[a-zA-Z]+|\[[^]]+\])*\.(push|splice|pop|shift|unshift|sort|reverse)\(" app/src`
   returns no matches — that pattern is direct mutation of live state instead
   of going through `dispatch`, including nested writes chained straight off
   `getState()` (e.g. `getState().tasks[0].done = true`). (Reading
   `getState().tasks` is fine; assigning to it or calling a mutating array
   method on it is not.) This only catches mutations written directly on the
   `getState()` expression — an aliased mutation split across statements
   (`const state = store.getState(); state.tasks.push(...)`) isn't caught;
   review those by eye, the same limitation `selectors.mdc` notes for
   destructured aliases.
5. `cd app && npm test` passes, including the existing immutability
   assertion in `reducer.test.ts` ("does not mutate the previous state").